---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import GcpStrip from '../../components/GcpStrip.astro';
import Screenshot from '../../components/Screenshot.astro';
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';

const entry = await getEntry('projects', 'reading-room');
const d = entry!.data;
---
<BaseLayout title="Reading Room" description={d.description} wide={true}>
  <p class="eyebrow mono small">Part of <a href="/products/linguistpro">LinguistPro</a></p>
  <h1>Reading Room</h1>
  <p class="lead">A bilingual library — the Hebrew canon with morphology on tap.</p>

  <div class="btn-row">
    <a href={d.url} class="btn btn-primary">Open the Reading Room</a>
    <a href="/products/linguistpro" class="btn btn-secondary">About LinguistPro</a>
  </div>

  <Screenshot
    src="/img/reading-room.webp"
    alt="The Reading Room Corpus view: a search bar, a 'Where to start' shelf of short easy texts, a 'Ready to read (796)' shelf of Hebrew works with machine-translation and provenance labels, and a Periods grid (Biblical, Medieval / Golden Age of Sepharad)."
    width={1040}
    height={880}
    caption="The Corpus view: 26,455 Project Ben-Yehuda works catalogued, with a prepared corpus of 796 works."
  />

  <h2>A whole library, not a handful of passages</h2>
  <p>
    Where the LinguistPro Studio is "bring any source," the Reading Room is "read the canon." It
    catalogues the public-domain Hebrew corpus as a bilingual, guided reading library — built on the open scholarship of
    <a href="https://benyehuda.org/">Project Ben-Yehuda</a> (the Hebrew-literature counterpart to Project
    Gutenberg), whose <a href="https://github.com/projectbenyehuda">open corpus</a> we catalogue, translate
    and enrich. It ships inside LinguistPro and shares its privacy-first engine, but it's its own learning
    surface, with its own way in.
  </p>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>26,455 works are catalogued</strong>, organized the way scholars think about Hebrew literature:
    a Period → Author → Work drill (in parity with benyehuda.org). You can move from the
    <strong>Biblical</strong> layer through the <strong>Medieval</strong> period (the Golden Age of
    Sepharad), the <strong>Haskalah</strong>, the <strong>Tehiya</strong> (the revival of Hebrew as a living
    language), the British <strong>Mandate</strong> era, and the <strong>Modern</strong> canon — across
    poetry, prose, articles, memoir, letters, fables and drama.
  </p>
  <p>
    The point isn't just access — it's a <strong>graded path</strong>. Full-text and in-reader search make
    the corpus navigable; Continue Reading and bookmarks preserve momentum. A "Where to start" shelf surfaces
    short texts built from high-frequency vocabulary, an easy way in; a personalized "next for you" engine
    recommends the next text just beyond your current level (the i+1 principle from second-language
    acquisition), computed entirely on-device from your own vocabulary. So a learner can begin with a
    few-line poem and end, text by text, reading the canon in the original. In each prepared work,
    translation, niqqud and morphology are available without pretending that every catalogued work has already been processed.
  </p>

  <h2>Honest, served on open</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The prepared corpus contains 796 works</strong>, with machine translation and Dicta niqqud
    plus <strong>provenance on every card</strong>. The latest rollout record documents 735 live works and
    61 held for re-enrichment; availability remains quality-gated. Translation, vocalization and audio
    status are labelled rather than silently dressed up as hand-curated canon. The rest of the 26,455-work
    catalogue is honestly marked as awaiting processing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Technically it stays true to LinguistPro's privacy-first design. The library is
    <strong>catalogue-driven and served-on-open</strong>: a thin index is cached up front, and a work only
    materializes into browser-local storage only when opened, so a 26,455-work catalogue never weighs
    down the device. Tap any word for offline morphological analysis; context mode can disambiguate a
    specific sentence. Reading remains local by default; account sync is a separate opt-in capability.
  </p>

  <h2>From a word in a book to a durable memory</h2>
  <div class="feature-pairs">
    <article>
      <h3>Honest morphology on tap</h3>
      <p>See root, binyan, part of speech, meaning, inflection tables, alternatives and provenance. Ambiguity is shown, not hidden.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Scaffolding that fades</h3>
      <p>Adaptive niqqud reveal and vocabulary-aware recommendations support the first read without permanently replacing the original.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Review in the source sentence</h3>
      <p>FSRS-6 schedules saved words and brings them back inside the sentence where they were learned.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>One personal note</h3>
      <p>A word saved in the Reading Room opens the same canonical note in the Studio, while personal wording stays separate from machine reference text.</p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <h2>Built on Google Cloud</h2>
  <p>
    Gemini does the translation of the corpus into Russian (and beyond), and Cloud Translation v3 backs the
    neural translation pipeline — the heavy compute that makes a 26k-work bilingual library feasible for a
    solo team.
  </p>
  <GcpStrip services={d.gcpServices} />

  <h2 id="physics-year1">The first professional corpus is live</h2>
  <div class="status-note">
    <p class="eyebrow">In production · 9 chapters · 74 problems</p>
    <p>
      <strong>“Physics — Year 1 Problem Book”</strong> is an open Hebrew–Russian study corpus for
      first-year technical-college students. This is no longer a roadmap promise: all 74 cards are
      available now in the Reading Room and pinned to one immutable, reviewed edition.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="feature-pairs">
    <article>
      <h3>Read the problem at four levels</h3>
      <p>Hebrew, Hebrew with niqqud, transliteration and Russian translation sit side by side. Tap a word to open its morphology card.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Keep the source attached</h3>
      <p>Each problem keeps its source PDF in a bounded in-app viewer, tied to that exact card.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Check the answer without an early spoiler</h3>
      <p>“Check answer” reveals the short reviewed result below the condition, after the learner has had room to solve it.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Follow a complete exam solution</h3>
      <p>“Understand and solve” opens a beginner bridge, Given, Find, SI conversion, physical model, base laws, formula derivation, sequential arithmetic, checks and final answer.</p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <p>
    Solutions were derived afresh, checked against the answer key and handwritten work, and every
    discovered discrepancy was resolved and recorded. Formulae use semantic subscripts and visible
    multiplication signs, so notation does not depend on guesswork. The result is written for a
    first-year learner who needs to reconstruct the reasoning, not merely see numbers inserted into a final formula.
  </p>

  <h2>One reviewed version for the learner and a connected agent</h2>
  <p>
    The interface and read-only agent access use the same material, pinned to the exact edition, item
    and SHA-256. With explicit consent, a connected AI assistant can therefore explain the approved
    walkthrough instead of improvising, without receiving notes, grades or learning history. The agent
    route remains a closed owner pilot, not a claim of general MCP availability.
  </p>

  <div class="btn-row">
    <a href="https://linguistpro.kolosei.com/library.html?public_corpus=physics-year1-problems" class="btn btn-primary">Open the Physics problem book</a>
    <a href={d.url} class="btn btn-secondary">All Reading Room corpora</a>
    <a href="/technology" class="btn btn-secondary">How we use Google Cloud</a>
  </div>

  <p class="small muted">
    This is an independent study aid for learners; Kolosei claims no affiliation, endorsement or accreditation by a college or the original problem-book publisher. Further technical subjects remain on the roadmap.
  </p>

</BaseLayout>
